---
description: User-facing text and URLs in Norwegian; developer-only content in English
alwaysApply: true
---

# Language: Norwegian for users, English for developers and AI chat

## Norwegian (Bokmål) — everything the end user can see

Use **Norwegian** for:

- **UI copy**: labels, buttons, headings, placeholders, empty states, toasts, dialogs, form validation messages shown in the app
- **SEO & marketing**: page titles, meta descriptions, alt text on images, legal/consumer-facing pages
- **URLs users see**: route path segments, slugs, and human-readable URL parts (e.g. `/produkter`, `/handlekurv`, `/kasse` — not `/products`, `/cart`, `/checkout`)
- **User-facing errors**: messages shown when something fails for the customer (keep technical detail out or behind “contact support”)

Prefer consistent Norwegian terminology across the product.

## English — developers only

Use **English** for:

- **Code**: variable names, function names, types, file names (unless the file is purely locale content)
- **Comments and JSDoc** in source
- **Git commits**, internal READMEs aimed at the team, API field names if they mirror a backend contract in English
- **Console logs**, debug strings, Sentry/internal error codes (user still sees Norwegian in the UI)
- **Non-user env vars**, config keys, feature flags
- **AI assistant chat responses** in Cursor (analysis, planning, status updates, and explanations to the team)

## Scope clarification (important)

This rule controls **product/storefront output**, not the language used by the AI assistant in conversation.

- Apply Norwegian only to text that end customers see in the app/site.
- Keep assistant-to-developer communication in English by default.
- If the team explicitly requests Norwegian in chat, follow that request for that conversation.

## Quick check

| Seen by customer? | Language   |
|-------------------|------------|
| Yes (UI, URL, email to user) | Norwegian |
| No (code, logs, dev docs)    | English   |

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD
const addToCart = () => { ... }; // English identifier
toast.success("Lagt i handlekurven"); // Norwegian for user

// Route: /handlekurv (Norwegian slug in app router)
```

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — English UI for end users
<Button>Add to cart</Button>
```

If a string is ambiguous (e.g. shared between admin and storefront), use the audience that actually sees it: storefront → Norwegian; internal admin tools for devs → English is OK if only staff use them.
